<FieldGuide title="Drying and storing specimens" description="How to dry and store fungal specimens so their DNA survives: temperature, timing, keeping collections apart and storage, from published protocols." eyebrow="FIELD RESEARCH TOOLKIT / DRYING & STORAGE" heading="Dry it right, <em>or the DNA is gone.</em>" lead="A specimen you can sequence in five years starts in the dehydrator tonight. Here’s what the protocols say, and why." tourKey="field-drying" tour={tour} sources={sources} current="drying">

<section id="why">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Why it matters</p>
  <h2>The dried specimen is the evidence.</h2>
  <p>Once a mushroom is dried and labelled, it becomes a voucher: the physical record someone can go back to, re-measure, re-sequence or name. A photo can’t be sequenced. A rotten or overheated specimen often can’t either.</p>
  <p>Two things work against you. <strong>Heat</strong> breaks DNA down, so drying too hot ruins a specimen even though it looks fine.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> <strong>Moisture</strong> lets the specimen rot or mould while it dries, and goes on damaging DNA after it’s dry if it’s stored damp.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup> Start drying as soon as you can after collecting.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup></p>
</section>

<section id="temperature">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">The temperature</p>
  <h2>Warm, not hot. Use a dehydrator with a thermostat.</h2>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>110–120 °F (43–50 °C)</h3><p>The FunDiS recommendation, for a dehydrator with temperature control.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup></p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Never above 155 °F (68 °C)</h3><p>FunDiS warns that temperatures above this can damage DNA.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> Many food dehydrators go higher than that, so check the dial.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Lower is gentler</h3><p>Other protocols go lower: 40–45 °C in a Queensland manual,<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">4</a></sup> and 30 °C for 48 hours in a 2022 study of DNA quality, which also cites 35 °C for 48 hours.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup> Lower settings take longer, so the dehydrator is full for longer.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="mv-guide-note"><p><strong>Where the sources differ:</strong> they agree that high heat is the risk. They don’t agree on one best setting, so “low and steady, and never hot” is the safe reading. A 2017 comparison of drying methods for mushroom DNA is listed below if you want the detail.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">6</a></sup></p></div>
  <div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: the dehydrator, with the thermostat set</div>
</section>

<section id="when-dry">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">When it’s done</p>
  <h2>“Cracker dry”: it snaps, it doesn’t bend.</h2>
  <p>FunDiS describes a finished specimen as cracker dry, or as dry as potato chips. That usually takes 12 to 48 hours, depending on how big and how wet the mushroom was.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup><sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">2</a></sup> A thick bolete stem takes far longer than a small <em>Mycena</em>, so check each collection rather than the whole rack at once.</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-do">
    <div><h3>Done</h3><ul><li>Snaps cleanly when bent</li><li>Feels light, with no cool or soft spots</li><li>The thickest part (stem base, bulb, cap centre) is dry too</li></ul></div>
    <div><h3>Not yet</h3><ul><li>Bends or feels leathery</li><li>The centre of the stem or cap is still soft</li><li>Feels heavier than it looks</li></ul></div>
  </div>
  <p>If you slice a large specimen to help it dry, keep every piece together with the same number.</p>
</section>

<section id="rack">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">On the rack</p>
  <h2>The dehydrator is where collections get mixed up.</h2>
  <p>FunDiS advises keeping contact between species to a minimum and labelling specimens on the rack.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">1</a></sup> Specimens that touch can pass tissue and spores to each other, and a single swapped label undoes everything else you did right.</p>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Loading</span><p>Put each collection in its own spot, with space around it, and its number (the corner of its field slip, or a printed label) right beside it.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Mixed sizes</span><p>Put the slow, thick collections where you can reach them without disturbing the rest, because they’ll stay on after the others come off.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Unloading</span><p>Bag one collection at a time, with its label, before you touch the next. Never take several off the rack and sort them afterwards.</p></li>
  </ol>
  <div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: a loaded rack, with each collection’s number beside it</div>
  <p>More on numbering and keeping things matched: <a href="/investigate/field/tracking/">Specimen tracking</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="storage">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Storage</p>
  <h2>Keep it dry once it’s dry.</h2>
  <p>Dried fungi absorb moisture from the air, and that moisture degrades DNA. FunDiS recommends adding desiccant if the relative humidity where you store specimens is above 50%.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup></p>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Seal it</h3><p>A sealed bag for each collection, with its label inside the bag, not only written on the outside.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Know your humidity</h3><p>An inexpensive hygrometer tells you whether your storage space is above 50%. If it is, add desiccant (silica gel) to the bags or the box.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup><sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">5</a></sup></p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Store it where you can find it</h3><p>If you send tissue for sequencing, storing bags by plate number means any result leads you straight back to the specimen. The <a href="/investigate/plate-tagger/">Plate Tagger</a> writes the plate onto each iNaturalist observation.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="routine">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">How I do it</p>
  <h2>My setup</h2>
  <div class="mv-guide-note"><p><strong>Donn:</strong> your dehydrator model and setting, how many collections fit per tray, how long a typical load takes, and how you store bags. This box is a placeholder until you send those details.</p></div>
  <p>Planning how many collections your dehydrator can handle in a week? Try the <a href="/investigate/collection-planner/">Collection Planner</a>.</p>
</section>

</FieldGuide>
